WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.184 --> 00:00:02.880
Film Courage: What’s the first&nbsp;
step for writing a screenplay?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.000
Phil Hawkins, Writer/Director:&nbsp;
The first step is usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:18.320
an idea of plot or story. I don’t really start&nbsp;
with a character. I admire people that do that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:30.560
But I’m very much like Stanislavski, kind of&nbsp;
outside-in-kind of thing – find the shoes and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.560
you’ll find the person as opposed to the other&nbsp;
way around kind of thing if that makes sense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:43.040
So I have an idea or a concept or a story I want&nbsp;
to tell and in writing it I find the characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.760
I find the story and then when I know&nbsp;
that it’s working whether that’s on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.840
outline perspective or whether it’s in a draft&nbsp;
perspective or whether it’s draft one or draft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:01:00.320
ten, when the characters suddenly start doing&nbsp;
things I don’t want them to do then I know I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.800
got the characters right and what I mean by&nbsp;
that is I can be writing and I can have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:13.520
outline and I can know where I’m going and then&nbsp;
that character will just start to say something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.840
out of my fingers and it will start to want&nbsp;
to go through that door instead of that door.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.400
I’m like well okay let’s go there and see what&nbsp;
happens you know and it’s I think it’s partly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.880
enjoying being flexible as you&nbsp;
write and partly I think it’s like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:39.600
my very basic drama acting training in me is&nbsp;
that I can become those characters I write them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:45.120
in a way and I feel that something in&nbsp;
my being says well no I wouldn’t do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.760
as that character so it will be your plot needs&nbsp;
something to do but your character might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.160
then you get into that interesting&nbsp;
area of like well okay let’s explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.080
what’s down that corridor or what’s that way&nbsp;
and you know you want those characters to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:15.040
so that can be interesting. That can be a really&nbsp;
pleasurable side of writing. I love structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:23.360
I always start with - I really like Save The Cat&nbsp;
[book by Blake Snyder] - controversial but I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:31.120
that if I am ever stuck or I am overwhelmed by&nbsp;
like Gosh, I’ve got to write like 1900 pages here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:40.080
how is my idea? My concept which sounds cool&nbsp;
as a paragraph how does that actually work as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:46.480
a feature film? Is it worth enough to be a feature&nbsp;
film? I do read that book or pick up that book or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.200
look at Hero’s Journey [Joseph Campbell] and I&nbsp;
will find the oh okay, that’s why, that’s how I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:03:00.400
going to tell the story. I may not adhere to it.&nbsp;
Part of knowing the rules is breaking the rules if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:07.440
rules exist. I don’t think they do but there’s a&nbsp;
reason why people like set-up and payoff. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:17.520
a reason why going back to the days of telling&nbsp;
a story around a campfire or at a party you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:23.600
tying a good anecdote like you don’t suddenly&nbsp;
come out with the ending it’s just that it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:32.000
satisfying. This idea of structure or the way we&nbsp;
tell stories is kind of some how ingrained in us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:43.440
so you need to understand that or you almost need&nbsp;
to play with the audience with that either to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.040
them feel comfortable or to completely subvert&nbsp;
the thing they think is going to happen. Like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:58.800
all the big twists that we can reel off all came&nbsp;
because the structure or the style of the film was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:05.680
setting up something we didn’t expect and then it&nbsp;
turned on its head and I’m thinking about the M.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:13.360
Night Shyamalan’s THE SIXTH SENSE and that kind of&nbsp;
things is because as an audience we’re very astute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:20.080
to structure whether that’s subconsciously or&nbsp;
consciously that we always want to try and be one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.360
step ahead so when a film pulls the rug it’s very&nbsp;
satisfying because it’s like we didn’t see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:33.840
coming. I know some people did see that coming.&nbsp;
I didn’t, I loved it. That’s how I would start,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:42.240
from a concept, start to apply it to some sort&nbsp;
of structure and then let the characters speak.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.040
Film Courage: When you were writing&nbsp;
THE LAST SHOWING did you actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.840
act out Stuart Lloyd? Did you become Stuart&nbsp;
for a little bit and say you know what,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.920
he wouldn’t have said that, he&nbsp;
would have done it this way?

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:02.400
Phil: Yes I did. There’s a lot of talking to&nbsp;
myself in my office and he’s a quite angry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:11.120
character. More so maybe now because I’ve got&nbsp;
sleeping kids downstairs I will act it in my head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:19.680
but I do like to read things out especially&nbsp;
long dialogue or long monologues or passages if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:27.680
try and find the flow because if you try and&nbsp;
write how I am talking right now in a screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.240
it would not be how you would&nbsp;
expect to write it. Firstly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.880
I don’t put enough full stops or commas in the way&nbsp;
I talk. I just go on in a stream of consciousness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.760
But if that’s the character actually&nbsp;
talking it out helps you find that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:50.400
because there is nothing worse than that all&nbsp;
the characters sound the same. It’s like why,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.960
how? Why do they sound the same and if you can&nbsp;
do things like that like punctuation-wise or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:03.680
flow-wise and you’ve acted it out then hopefully&nbsp;
you can find that in you through the writing.

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.960
Film Courage: [Referring to LAST SHOWING movie]&nbsp;
So in starting with a screenplay you come up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:14.240
with an idea - so a projectionist who feels like&nbsp;
he’s being replaced, new technology has come in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.800
younger workers. He’s kind of given this&nbsp;
sort of throwaway job just to keep him on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:27.840
and then you kind of develop who he is, his&nbsp;
psychology and then after that, what’s next?

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.840
Phil: Well hopefully I’ve&nbsp;
got a script by that point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:43.680
Sometimes I write very detailed outlines.&nbsp;
Sometimes I can’t wait to get started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:52.320
and I will write a rough outline or I will even&nbsp;
just bullet point a rough structure and see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:07:06.800
how we go. In writing LAST SHOWING which seems&nbsp;
really fundamental now to the film he is trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.760
to make (kind of the film within the film) but&nbsp;
that whole idea of him wanting to be the hero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:22.480
didn’t really come about I’m making a horror&nbsp;
film. I’m going to teach these people a lesson and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:34.640
record it in some sort of weird bad movie style&nbsp;
thing and I can’t remember whether he…I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:42.800
he actually got caught. He got hurt - he did go&nbsp;
actually in an early draft. He did go but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:52.880
felt too cliche. It was talking about that thing&nbsp;
structure, that’s what the audience maybe wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:57.520
but actually in writing Stuart [played by actor&nbsp;
Robert Englund] I felt myself sympathizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:03.680
with him more than the other characters.&nbsp;
When people see the film I think people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:12.080
see that and actually one of the film reviews (and&nbsp;
I must have paid him a lot of money to say this)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.680
but he said it was akin to ONE HOUR PHOTO and&nbsp;
Robin Williams. He’s like a really bad guy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.760
and you don’t agree with what he’s&nbsp;
doing but you understand what he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:25.840
doing and it’s a really funny gray area&nbsp;
and there’s tension there. Now my film is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:32.560
naught point one, point one percent of the&nbsp;
wonderful movie that is ONE HOUR PHOTO because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:39.040
that’s one of my favorites so that’s obviously&nbsp;
coming through as well in the writing (an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:46.960
homage to that as well). But I think when Stuart&nbsp;
started talking to me I realized that I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:51.920
felt that what he was doing was right and it&nbsp;
was important to teach these people a lesson and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:59.280
he wasn’t the bad guy because bad people don’t&nbsp;
think they’re doing bad things. So it’s fun to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:04.400
explore that and then I thought oh well that’s&nbsp;
interesting because if the film is commenting on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.680
protagonist versus antagonist relations in itself

